    </h1>

    <!-- 45-60 word Quick Answer box with green accent border -->
    <div class="bg-emerald-950/20 border-l-4 border-l-emerald-500 border-y border-r border-emerald-500/30 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8 backdrop-blur-sm shadow-xl">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer (The High-Throughput Blueprint)
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Handling 100,000 GitHub webhooks per second requires decoupling ingress from processing via an asynchronous streaming architecture. A lightweight FastAPI gateway verifies HMAC-SHA256 signatures in under 4ms and flushes raw events into Apache Kafka or Redpanda partitioned by repository ID, ensuring strict per-repository ordering, sub-second buffer elasticity, and zero dropped payloads during traffic bursts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-8 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-base">
      <!-- Section 1: Architecture Diagram & System Flow -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">1. High-Throughput Architecture Blueprint</h2>
        <p>
          At enterprise scale (CI/CD platforms, code security scanners, developer tooling), GitHub webhook traffic is bursty. During morning deployment windows or monorepo pushes, thousands of git refs trigger simultaneous deliveries. GitHub enforces a strict <strong>10-second timeout</strong>: if your webhook receiver executes synchronous DB writes or heavy business logic, requests back up, timeouts cascade, and GitHub disables the webhook integration.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The solution is a three-tier decoupled streaming architecture:
        </p>

        <!-- Architecture Diagram Box -->
        <div class="my-6 p-6 bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 rounded-2xl font-mono text-xs text-slate-300 overflow-x-auto">
          <div class="text-slate-400 font-bold mb-3 uppercase tracking-wider text-[11px] flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>📊</span> Ingestion & Streaming Dataflow
          </div>
          <pre class="leading-snug text-cyan-300">
┌─────────────────┐       HTTPS POST        ┌────────────────────────────────┐
│  GitHub Servers │ ──────────────────────> │  FastAPI Ingestion Gateway     │
│  (100k req/sec) │                         │  - HMAC-SHA256 Verification    │
└─────────────────┘                         │  - Memory Buffer & Batching    │
                                            └───────────────┬────────────────┘
                                                            │ Async Producer (librdkafka)
                                                            ▼ (Key = repo_id)
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Apache Kafka / Redpanda Cluster                         │
│  Topic: github-events-raw (64 Partitions, Replication Factor: 3)           │
│  ├── Partition 0: Repo 1024, Repo 88201... (Strict Chronological Order)   │
│  ├── Partition 1: Repo 4091, Repo 99212...                                │
│  └── Partition N: ...                                                      │
└───────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                │ Consumer Group (Parallel Workers)
                                ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Downstream Processing & DLQ Replay Loop                   │
│                                                                            │
│   ┌─────────────────────┐   Exception   ┌──────────────────────────────┐   │
│   │ Processing Workers  │ ────────────> │ Retry Topic (Exponential)    │   │
│   │ (DB write, CI hook) │               └──────────────┬───────────────┘   │
│   └─────────────────────┘                              │ Max Exhausted     │
│                                                        ▼                   │
│                                         ┌──────────────────────────────┐   │
│                                         │ Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)      │   │
│                                         │ - S3/Iceberg Raw Archive     │   │
│                                         │ - Admin Replay Dashboard     │   │
│                                         └──────────────────────────────┘   │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
          </pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: FastAPI Ingestion Gateway Code Snippet -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">
          2. FastAPI Ingestion Gateway Implementation
        </h2>
        <p>
          The gateway must accomplish exactly two tasks with zero blocking: verify the <code class="text-orange-300 font-mono">X-Hub-Signature-256</code> header using constant-time comparison, and write the raw payload to Kafka using the repository ID as the partition key:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 my-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-200 overflow-x-auto">
          <pre is:raw><code>import hmac
import hashlib
import json
import asyncio
from fastapi import FastAPI, Request, Header, HTTPException, status
from fastapi.responses import JSONResponse
from aiokafka import AIOKafkaProducer

app = FastAPI(title="GitHub Webhook Ingestion Engine")

WEBHOOK_SECRET = b"production_shared_webhook_secret_key_2026"
KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP = "redpanda-cluster.internal:9092"
TOPIC_NAME = "github-events-raw"

producer: AIOKafkaProducer | None = None

@app.on_event("startup")
async def startup_event():
    global producer
    producer = AIOKafkaProducer(
        bootstrap_servers=KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP,
        compression_type="lz4",
        linger_ms=5,             # 5ms micro-batching for extreme throughput
        max_batch_size=131072,   # 128 KB buffer per partition
        acks=1                   # Leader acknowledgment for low latency
    )
    await producer.start()

@app.on_event("shutdown")
async def shutdown_event():
    if producer:
        await producer.stop()

def verify_github_signature(payload_body: bytes, signature_header: str | None) -> bool:
    if not signature_header or not signature_header.startswith("sha256="):
        return False
    expected_hash = hmac.new(WEBHOOK_SECRET, payload_body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
    received_hash = signature_header[7:]
    return hmac.compare_digest(expected_hash, received_hash)

@app.post("/webhooks/github")
async def receive_github_webhook(
    request: Request,
    x_hub_signature_256: str = Header(None),
    x_github_event: str = Header(None),
    x_github_delivery: str = Header(None)
):
    # 1. Read raw body bytes (<1ms)
    body_bytes = await request.body()

    # 2. Verify cryptographic HMAC signature in constant time
    if not verify_github_signature(body_bytes, x_hub_signature_256):
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=status.HTTP_401_UNAUTHORIZED,
            detail="Invalid GitHub webhook HMAC-SHA256 signature"
        )

    # 3. Extract Partition Key (repository ID) without heavy parsing
    try:
        raw_json = json.loads(body_bytes)
        repo_id = str(raw_json.get("repository", {}).get("id", "default"))
    except Exception:
        repo_id = "default"

    # 4. Asynchronously push to Kafka topic
    event_metadata = {
        "delivery_id": x_github_delivery,
        "event_type": x_github_event,
        "payload": raw_json
    }

    await producer.send(
        topic=TOPIC_NAME,
        key=repo_id.encode("utf-8"),
        value=json.dumps(event_metadata).encode("utf-8")
    )

    # 5. Immediate 202 Accepted response back to GitHub (<10ms)
    return JSONResponse(
        status_code=status.HTTP_202_ACCEPTED,
        content={"status": "enqueued", "delivery_id": x_github_delivery}
    )</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Partitioning Strategy & Ordering Guarantees -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">
          3. Partitioning Strategy: Eliminating Cross-Repository Contention
        </h2>
        <p>
          A common architectural mistake is partitioning by <code class="text-orange-300 font-mono">x_github_delivery</code> (random UUID) or round-robin. While this distributes load evenly, it destroys message ordering. If an engineer pushes a commit and immediately opens a Pull Request, random partitioning allows the PR event to be processed before the commit push event, creating race conditions.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          By utilizing <strong class="text-white">repository ID</strong> (<code class="text-cyan-300 font-mono">repository.id</code>) as the message key:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2">
          <li>All actions within a specific repository are routed deterministically to the same partition.</li>
          <li>Within a partition, events are strictly FIFO (First In, First Out).</li>
          <li>Separate repositories do not block one another, ensuring high concurrency across organizations.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Consumer Offset Tuning Table -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">
          4. Consumer Offset & Batch Tuning Parameters (100k req/sec)
        </h2>
