          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 border-b border-slate-800 text-[11px] uppercase font-mono text-slate-400">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3">Kafka Parameter</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-orange-400">Recommended Value</th>
                <th class="p-3 text-cyan-400">Production Rationale</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800/60 font-mono text-xs">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-bold">fetch.min.bytes</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">65,536 (64 KB)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300 font-sans">Forces broker to batch records, saving CPU syscalls during bursts.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-bold">fetch.max.wait.ms</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">50 ms</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300 font-sans">Upper bound latency if batch size isn't met; balances throughput and real-time feel.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-bold">max.poll.records</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">500</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300 font-sans">Prevents worker starvation and guarantees completion within poll timeout.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-bold">enable.auto.commit</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-rose-400 font-bold">false</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300 font-sans">Manual offset commits after successful DB transaction to guarantee at-least-once.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-bold">session.timeout.ms</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-cyan-400">45,000 ms</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300 font-sans">Protects against false-positive consumer rebalances during garbage collection.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-bold">compression.type</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">lz4</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300 font-sans">Ultra-fast decompression speeds with 60% compression ratio on JSON payloads.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: DLQ Replay Pattern -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">
          5. Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) & Redrive Pattern
        </h2>
        <p>
          When downstream workers encounter unrecoverable errors (schema migration drifts, database deadlocks), messages should not stall the primary partition.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Instead, implement a tiered dead-letter retry topology:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2">
          <li><strong>Immediate Retry (Local):</strong> Attempt 3 quick retries with 100ms jittered backoff in worker memory.</li>
          <li><strong>Delayed Topic (<code class="text-orange-300 font-mono">webhooks-retry-5m</code>):</strong> If local retry fails, emit the record to a delayed topic with a timestamp header. A timer service replays after 5 minutes.</li>
          <li><strong>Permanent DLQ (<code class="text-rose-400 font-mono">webhooks-dead-letter</code>):</strong> Exhausted messages (after 5 attempts) are routed to long-term storage in Apache Iceberg or AWS S3 with full origin headers. Once bug fixes deploy, an operations engineer triggers a CLI redrive back into the primary queue.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>

